INSERT INTO saturday_ready (release_date, theme, scripture_reference, scripture_text, preparation_body, three_commitments, prayer, is_premium)
VALUES

-- Week 1: Integrity Under Pressure (2026-08-07)
('2026-08-07',
 'Walking onto the Lot With Integrity',
 'Proverbs 11:3',
 'The integrity of the upright guides them, but the crookedness of the treacherous destroys them.',
 'Saturday is the biggest day of the week on the lot. Traffic peaks. Tensions run high. The pressure to close is at its maximum — and so is the temptation to cut corners. Proverbs says integrity guides the upright. It is not just a moral trait; it is a navigational tool. When the pressure mounts and you are not sure what to do, integrity becomes your compass. It points north when every other signal on the floor is spinning. This Saturday, before you take your first up, decide who you are going to be. Not what you are going to sell — who you are going to be. The numbers will come or they will not. Your character is the one thing no customer and no manager can take from you. Walk onto the lot determined to keep it intact. Let integrity guide you through the day — not as a burden but as the path that leads to life.',
 '["I will not inflate a payment, omit a material fact, or misrepresent a vehicle''s condition — even if a manager asks me to.", "I will treat every customer with patience and respect, regardless of whether I think they will buy.", "I will pause at least once during the day to pray — for a customer, a coworker, or my own heart."]',
 'Lord, Saturday is coming. The pressure will be real. The temptations will be specific. Go before me onto the lot. Let integrity be my guide. When I am tempted to cut corners, remind me whose I am. When I am frustrated, give me patience. When I close a deal, let me close it honestly. When I lose one, let me lose it with grace. Protect my character and my witness.',
 true),

-- Week 2: Serving Difficult Customers (2026-08-14)
('2026-08-14',
 'The Customer Who Tests Every Nerve',
 'Romans 12:17-18',
 'Repay no one evil for evil, but give thought to do what is honorable in the sight of all. If possible, so far as it depends on you, live peaceably with all.',
 'Some customers walk onto the lot carrying more than a trade-in. They carry frustration, suspicion, and a readiness to treat you as the enemy before you have said a word. Your instinct will be to match their energy — to be short, to do the minimum, to write them off before they write you off. Paul''s instruction is difficult and specific: repay no one evil for evil. Do what is honorable. Live peaceably — as far as it depends on you. He acknowledges the limit. You cannot control how they treat you. But you can control what you give back. The customer who is rude to you may be facing a divorce, a medical bill, or a repossession you cannot see. Your patience may be the only kindness they encounter today. That is not just sales. That is ministry.',
 '["I will not return rudeness with rudeness. I will respond to every customer with courtesy, even when it is not returned.", "I will remember that difficult customers are often carrying invisible burdens — and I will pray for one specific difficult customer by name.", "I will set a gracious boundary if a customer becomes abusive, without escalating the conflict."]',
 'Father, give me patience for the customer who tries my last nerve. Help me see them the way you see them — as someone you love, carrying burdens I cannot see. Let me be a peacemaker on the lot tomorrow, not a combatant. Protect my heart from hardening.',
 true),

-- Week 3: Rest When Exhausted (2026-08-21)
('2026-08-21',
 'You Do Not Have to Hustle Until You Break',
 'Psalm 127:2',
 'It is in vain that you rise up early and go late to rest, eating the bread of anxious toil; for he gives to his beloved sleep.',
 'The car business normalizes exhaustion. Six-day weeks. Bell-to-bell shifts. The phone that never stops ringing. You learn to wear fatigue like a badge of honor — proof that you are grinding, that you want it badly enough. The Psalmist calls this "anxious toil" and names it as vanity. Not the work itself — work is good, given by God. But the anxious, desperate, never-enough kind of work that steals sleep and cannot trust God with the outcome. "He gives to his beloved sleep." Not as a reward for productivity — as a gift. Sleep is an act of trust. It declares that the dealership will survive without you for eight hours, and that your worth is not measured by how exhausted you are. If you are running on fumes as you prepare for Saturday, that is not dedication. It is a warning light.',
 '["I will not come in early or stay late tomorrow unless there is a genuine, time-sensitive reason — not out of anxiety.", "I will take a real lunch break — away from the floor, off my phone — and use five minutes of it to pray.", "I will go to bed tonight at a reasonable hour, trusting God with what I cannot control."]',
 'Lord, I confess how often I wear exhaustion as a badge of virtue. Teach me to rest as an act of trust. Help me sleep tonight, releasing the deals that depend on you and not on my anxiety. Let tomorrow''s work be steady, not frantic.',
 true),

-- Week 4: Family Boundaries (2026-08-28)
('2026-08-28',
 'The People Waiting at Home',
 'Ephesians 5:25',
 'Husbands, love your wives, as Christ loved the church and gave himself up for her.',
 'The dealership demands your presence on Saturdays — the biggest sales day of the week. But Saturday is also the day your family is home. Your spouse is managing the household alone. Your kids have games you are missing. The tension is real, and it does not resolve neatly. Paul''s command to husbands to love as Christ loved the church sets a standard that applies to every relationship, not just marriage. Christ''s love was sacrificial — he gave himself. That means your family deserves your best energy, not just the leftovers after the floor has taken everything. This does not mean quitting your job or refusing to work Saturdays. It means being intentional about what you bring home. Can you leave the frustration on the lot? Can you be present for the hour you have rather than resenting the hours you do not? Tomorrow, the people at home need you, not just your paycheck.',
 '["I will leave work at work — no CRM checks at the dinner table, no replaying deals in my head while my family talks to me.", "I will give my family at least one uninterrupted hour of my full attention tomorrow — no phone, no distractions.", "I will tell my spouse or a close family member one specific thing I appreciate about them before the day ends."]',
 'Jesus, you gave yourself for the people you love. Help me give my family more than my tired leftovers. Protect my heart from the resentment that builds when the floor takes what my family needs. Let me be present for the people who matter most.',
 true),

-- Week 5: Ethical Dilemmas (2026-09-04)
('2026-09-04',
 'When the Right Thing Costs You',
 'Daniel 3:17-18',
 'Our God whom we serve is able to deliver us from the burning fiery furnace, and he will deliver us out of your hand, O king. But if not, be it known to you, O king, that we will not serve your gods.',
 'Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego faced a choice: bow to the idol or burn. Their answer is one of the most remarkable statements of faith in Scripture. "God is able to deliver us. But even if he does not, we will not bow." On the sales floor, the furnace looks different. It might be a manager asking you to lie about a vehicle''s history. A finance director pushing you to hide fees. A culture that rewards cutting corners. Saying no could cost you a deal, a relationship, maybe your job. The three men in Daniel did not know they would be saved when they refused to bow. They refused anyway. Not because they were certain of rescue, but because they were certain of whom they served. What furnace are you facing this Saturday? What cost are you afraid of? Walk in knowing that the God you serve is able — and that obedience is not contingent on a happy ending.',
 '["I will identify one ethical pressure point I am likely to face tomorrow and decide in advance how I will respond.", "I will not participate in any conversation or practice that I know is dishonest — even if staying silent would be easier.", "I will pray for courage to do what is right, regardless of the outcome, trusting God with the consequences."]',
 'God, the furnace is real. I am afraid of what obedience might cost me. Give me the faith of Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego — the courage to say no even when I cannot see how the story ends. You are able to deliver. But even if you do not, I will not bow.',
 true),

-- Week 6: Fear of Failure (2026-09-11)
('2026-09-11',
 'When the Numbers Scare You',
 'Isaiah 41:10',
 'Fear not, for I am with you; be not dismayed, for I am your God; I will strengthen you, I will help you, I will uphold you with my righteous right hand.',
 'The fear that grips a salesperson is specific and relentless. What if I do not sell anything this month? What if I cannot pay the mortgage? What if my manager finally decides I am not cut out for this? Isaiah speaks into that fear with a promise that is not a platitude. God does not say "do not be afraid because everything will work out." He says "do not be afraid because I am with you." His presence is the answer, not a guarantee of outcomes. He promises strength, help, and being upheld — not a guaranteed commission check. This reframes Saturday entirely. You are not walking onto the lot alone, hoping your skills are enough. You are walking onto the lot with the God who holds the universe, and he has promised to strengthen you. The outcome is in his hands. Your job is to show up, do honest work, and trust him with the rest.',
 '["I will not check the leaderboard or compare my numbers to anyone else tomorrow. I will focus on serving well, not on the score.", "When fear rises — and it will — I will pause and pray Isaiah 41:10 silently: ''Fear not, for I am with you.''", "I will do one act of genuine service tomorrow that has no chance of leading to a deal, just because it is right."]',
 'Lord, I am afraid. Afraid of failing, afraid of not measuring up, afraid of what a bad month could mean. Replace my fear with trust — not trust in my abilities, but trust in your presence. You are with me. That is enough.',
 true),

-- Week 7: Comparison (2026-09-18)
('2026-09-18',
 'The Guy in the Next Tower',
 'Galatians 6:4-5',
 'But let each one test his own work, and then his reason to boast will be in himself alone and not in his neighbor. For each will have to bear his own load.',
 'He got the fresh up. He closed three deals this week. The managers love him. His numbers are always at the top of the board. Comparison is the thief of joy, and the car business is designed to feed it. The board is public. The rankings are visible. The message is clear: your worth is your production relative to everyone else. Paul cuts through this with a liberating command: test your own work. Not your work compared to the guy in the next tower — your work, before God. You have your own load to carry. He has his. Your calling is singular. His success does not diminish yours. Your slow week is not a verdict when set beside his hot streak. Walk onto the lot tomorrow freed from the exhausting work of comparison. Run your own race.',
 '["I will not compare my numbers to anyone else tomorrow. If my eyes drift to the board, I will redirect them.", "I will genuinely celebrate a coworker''s success — a closed deal, a happy customer — without letting it diminish my own sense of worth.", "I will focus on doing my own work with excellence, measuring it by faithfulness, not by comparison."]',
 'Jesus, I am tired of comparison. I measure my worth against people who are not me and callings that are not mine. Turn my eyes back to you. Give me the grace to run my own race without looking sideways. Let me celebrate others without envying them.',
 true),

-- Week 8: Walking Away from a Bad Deal (2026-09-25)
('2026-09-25',
 'The Courage to Let It Go',
 'Proverbs 16:8',
 'Better is a little with righteousness than great revenues with injustice.',
 'Every salesperson has faced it: the deal that feels wrong. The customer who cannot really afford the payment but the bank approved it anyway. The vehicle with a problem no one has disclosed. The manager pushing you to close something your conscience is flagging. The commission is real. The pressure to produce is real. And Proverbs says: better is a little with righteousness. Not "it will work out if you walk away." Not "a better deal is coming." Just: righteousness is better than revenue. Period. This is not prosperity gospel — it is the opposite. It is the willingness to have less if the alternative is having it wrongly. Walking away from a bad deal may not be rewarded. It may not be noticed. It may cost you money. And it is still the right thing to do. Tomorrow, you may face the choice. Decide in advance: you would rather have a clean conscience and a smaller check than a deal you cannot look at without shame.',
 '["If a deal requires me to compromise my integrity — lying, hiding, manipulating — I will walk away, even if it costs me the commission.", "I will not pressure a customer into a payment they cannot afford, regardless of what the bank approved.", "If I walk away from a deal tomorrow, I will not second-guess myself. I will thank God for the strength to do right."]',
 'Lord, give me the courage to walk away when walking away is the right thing to do. I do not want revenue that costs me my soul. Help me trust that you are my provider, not any single deal. Let me close tomorrow with a clean conscience, whatever the board says.',
 true);
